# Database / Migration Rules

Applies to everything under `migrations/`. Full table reference: spec/01-schema.md.

- UUID primary keys everywhere (`gen_random_uuid()` via `pgcrypto`, or application-
  generated UUIDv7 if you want time-ordered IDs — pick one and apply it consistently).
- Every table gets `created_at` / `updated_at` timestamps (default `now()`,
  `updated_at` maintained by trigger or application code — pick one approach and apply
  it everywhere, don't mix).
- `branch_id` is nullable on every table that references it (serials, stock_bulk,
  invoices, purchase_orders). This is intentional: multi-branch is optional. Never add a
  `NOT NULL` constraint on `branch_id`, and never write application logic that assumes
  it is always present.
- No enum values hardcoded redundantly across migrations and application code — define
  Postgres `ENUM` types (or `CHECK` constraints) for status fields (serial status,
  invoice status, RMA status, payment method) so the database itself rejects invalid
  states, and generate/mirror the same values as typed constants in Go/TS rather than
  retyping the list by hand in a second place.
- Foreign keys with explicit `ON DELETE` behavior chosen deliberately per relationship
  (e.g., `RESTRICT` on anything tied to a financial record like invoice_items; `CASCADE`
  only where losing child rows is actually correct). Never leave the default unconsidered.
- Index every foreign key column and every column used in a hot lookup path (serial
  number lookup, invoice number lookup, barcode scan lookup).
- Never edit a migration file that may have already run anywhere (local dev counts once
  shared with the team). Add a new migration to change course.
- Seed data must not assume any branch exists — branches are optional, so seed scripts
  should work with zero branch rows present.
